## What it does

The `packages/flags` [cartridge](/lore/cartridges) evaluates typed flags.
A flag declares its name, [schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary), default, owner, and removal date.

Handlers request the `Flags` [service](/lore/services-capture-dependencies) from core.
The Config [adapter](/lore/hexagonal-architecture) reads deployment configuration.
The memory adapter supplies rules and writable values for tests.

The KV adapter owns its namespace through [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy).
Missing KV values use Config values or declared defaults.

- `listFlags` lists names, owners, and removal dates.
- `getFlag` evaluates one flag for an optional subject.
- Allow-lists select a value for named subjects.
- Percentage rules use a stable hash of the flag name and subject.
- The first matching rule wins. Missing subjects use the default.

`EVENTS_ENABLED` defaults to `false` and stays deploy-time Config.
Live KV writes cannot change this flag.

`EVENTS_IDENTITY_MODE` defaults to `daily`.
The Worker reads its live value for each captured request.
Config keeps the existing environment keys and boolean spellings.
An empty KV namespace preserves Config behavior.

Invalid Config stops initialization.
A failed KV read returns a typed error with its configured fallback recorded.

Previews use Config and declare no flags namespace.
The production Worker and content backend read KV.

[Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy) still selects analytics resources during deployment.
See [Analytics](/systems/analytics) for capture and identity behavior.

## The standard

- Flag values pass their declared schemas.
- Every declaration names an owner and a `removeBy` date.
- Lint rejects declarations without a literal removal date.
- The flags build rejects expired registered events flags with a typed repair message.
- Each subject keeps its rollout bucket when the rollout percentage changes.
- The lifecycle permits `declared` → `rollingOut` → `on` → `removed`.
- Declared [Effect](/lore/effect-basics) actors record lifecycle transitions. Removed flags stay removed.
- Each [capability](/systems/capabilities) shares its [contract](/systems/capabilities) across surfaces. JSON encoding is required for `getFlag`.

`setFlag` is an approval-gated capability on the CLI.
The CLI accepts `--yes` or asks for interactive approval.
Its default composition has no live writer and returns a named error.

The CLI records `local-cli` as a channel label.
Its caller has no authenticated person identity.

The public Worker, [RPC](/skills/learn-rat-stack) readers, and read-only sandbox expose no flag writes.
A live operator write [surface](/lore/one-schema-three-surfaces) and `listFlagChanges` remain future work.

Each available-store attempt records its caller, flag, observed old value, requested value, time, and outcome.
The journal stores an immutable Pending key before the approval gate.
A separate completion key records success, denial, failure, or interruption.
Both keys share an attempt id.

Missing completion means Pending with an unknown result.
A failed completion write returns a typed error; the flag may have changed.
Audit storage failures block only writes.
Config and default journals use composition-local memory.

KV changes may take 60 seconds or more to reach another location.
The observed old value may be stale.
KV cannot commit a flag value and its audit outcome in one transaction.
Unique journal keys avoid its one-write-per-second limit on each key.

Writes to the same flag can still hit that limit and return a typed error.

The lifecycle journal is scoped memory, with no durable state between processes.

## How to check

1. Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/flags test` for rollout properties, generated write histories, audit failures, and lifecycle histories.
2. Run `pnpm --filter @rat-stack/core exec vitest run test/patterns-rule.test.ts` for the removal-date [fence](/lore/the-fence).
3. Run `pnpm turbo run check test build --concurrency=1` for the full gate.
4. Inspect the deployment plan. Adding live flags declares one KV namespace and updates its Worker bindings.

To remove the cartridge:

1. Replace `apps/site/src/flags.ts` with core imports.
2. Export `flagsLayer = Flags.defaults(eventFlags)` and `liveFlagsLayer = flagsLayer`.
3. Remove the package dependency and its directory.

Callers keep their typed defaults.
The CLI retains its named read-only failure.
